 ## Speed
 A standing rule, not a one-off. Tests and checks run very often, so they must run **concurrently** and as swiftly as possible.
 - **What exists**: `z-nile/lib/par.sh` runs the independent static stages together (`par_start` / `par_wait`: buffered, timed, reported in start order); `tests/run` runs spec files in concurrent workers (`--jobs N`, `PICKMAN_JOBS`), slowest first by the durations it records in the home's `.cache/spec-durations.tsv` (files with no recorded time first, ties by path; c0154), so a long spec no longer starts late and holds the gate open at the end. Each result prints as its file finishes, and all runners for this user share eight machine-wide worker lanes (`--jobs` remains a per-run cap). A run in-process (`--jobs 1`, one file) neither reads nor writes the record. `PICKMAN_JOBS=1` restores serial runs when you suspect an ordering bug.
-- **Monitor continuously**: every gate run prints per-stage times, a `slowest:` line and `gate wall time:`, and whoever runs it looks at them. A stage or spec that dominates, or a time that creeps up between runs, is a gate defect: **optimise** it now if it is in your Touched modules, otherwise file a chore (`./zn new issue c gate-slow-<what> --project z-nile`). Never add a serial dependency between stages without a reason.
+- **Monitor continuously**: every gate run prints per-stage times, a `slowest:` line and `gate wall time:`, and whoever runs it looks at them. A stage or spec that dominates, or a time that creeps up between runs, is a gate defect: **optimise** it now if it is in your Touched modules, otherwise file a chore (`./zn new issue c gate-slow-<what> --project z-nile --filed-by "agent:<id>/gate-slow"`). Never add a serial dependency between stages without a reason.
 - **Writing specs for speed**: no wall-clock assertions and no fixed sleeps to let something finish (a bounded poll or rendezvous with a timeout is fine). Shell-heavy fixtures own a unique `.cache/<name>` (a literal `.cache/<dir>` in two spec files under `tests/` fails the gate, a mere mention counts, and `sim` is exempt). No shared mutable state across spec files. Prefer in-process Logic specs, and split or stub a spec that takes more than about a second. Two suites in one checkout queue on the runner's lock; separate worktrees run in parallel (see "Specs never touch the real repository").
 
